[h1]Heroes Evolve[/h1]

Your hero grows. He learns from every fight, earns his way into better armour,
and becomes someone over the years. The lords around him do not. In vanilla they
are handed a kit at eighteen and carry it, unchanged, to the grave, while their
skills sit still beside it.

Heroes Evolve gives them the life you have. Lords and companions learn from what
they do, go to market for better gear, and take it off each other when they win.
The world stops waiting for you and starts moving with you: the lord you beat at
twenty-five is not the man you will face at forty.

[h2]Main features[/h2]

[b]Nobody is left with nothing[/b]
[list]
[*]A lord can end up with nothing two ways. Some are born to it: they come of
age in civilian clothes carrying one sword, or with most of their skills still
at zero, and left alone they stay that way for life. Others are stripped by
whoever took them prisoner.
[*]Either way he has to get home, and what he rides in wearing is what he could
put together on the road: a village that took him in, a smith who owed his house
a favour.
[*]The kit follows the man: his culture, how his people actually fight, and what
he himself is good at, and a man of his years knows what a man of his years
should.
[*]It is a floor and not a gift. Plain, serviceable, enough to ride out again --
and everything above it he goes and buys himself.
[/list]

[b]Their own way[/b]
[list]
[*]Now and then a lord comes of age and fights unlike his people. Nearly all
take up the way their culture teaches; once in a while one does not, and that
man is armed for who he is rather than for the custom he was born into.
[*]A Vlandian crossbowman on foot. A Battanian skirmisher throwing javelins from
the saddle. A Khuzait archer who never took to the horse. A Sturgian
javelin-rider. An Imperial crossbowman.
[*]Always within what his people really field, read from their own troops, so it
works with modded cultures too. You will never meet a Vlandian archer, a
Battanian horse archer, or a mounted Nord.
[*]Uncommon enough that meeting one is worth remarking on, and the reason no
two campaigns field quite the same nobility.
[/list]

[b]Skills grow for life[/b]
[list]
[*]Lords and companions improve at the weapons they carry and the trades they
have focus in, year after year, toward a ceiling set by their own talent.
[*]A new campaign starts every lord where his years and talent put him: young
lords begin young and grow into their strength. Campaigns already under way are
left as they are.
[*]Every lord has a talent in each of three fields: war, management and the sea.
It is dealt at random, and the luckiest can outgrow any king; a crown won later
changes nothing. A lord TaleWorlds wrote by hand keeps his written sheet as a
floor, however the dice fell: he grows back onto it in his fifties and ends five
percent above it -- Caladog is still a prodigy with a blade. It is a multiplier:
a talented lord learns faster and climbs higher, an untalented one stops lower.
The three are independent -- a born warrior may be a hopeless steward -- so no
two lords turn out alike, and each keeps his talents for life.
[*]Talent in all three at once is rare -- two to four lords in a campaign,
prodigies. Even they peak below a well-trained hero of your own.
[*]It never runs away. A gifted lord climbs high; an ordinary one levels off.
[*]It reaches lords sitting in their castles and wanderers waiting in taverns --
so a companion hired late is worth more, and costs more.
[/list]

[b]Lords buy their own gear[/b]
[list]
[*]Entering a town, a lord may visit the market and buy better equipment with his
clan's own money, within what his skill lets him wear.
[*]He only ever improves what he already carries. A swordsman looks for a better
sword; he never comes back with a bow, never fills an empty slot, never climbs
onto a horse if he fought on foot, and rides a camel only if his people do.
[*]That keeps you in control of your own clan. Equip a companion, give him a
party, and he will improve that loadout -- never swap it for one you did not
choose.
[*]He dresses evenly. His worst piece of armour is replaced first, so you will
not see a lord in fine gloves and boots over cheap body armour.
[*]Wealth matters: a rich house outfits its lords better. A reserve is always held
back, so shopping never leaves a clan unable to pay its troops.
[/list]

[b]A caravan master buys his own armour[/b]
[list]
[*]He takes a commission on what his caravan brings in and shops out of that,
not out of your treasury. So a caravan that trades well arms the man running
it, and a poor one leaves him plain.
[*]He starts with nothing and can never spend more than he has earned. Good
armour is something he works up to over years, not something you hand him.
[*]The longer he survives, the better he ends up armed -- and surviving is his
own doing, since a caravan outruns bandits on its master's scouting and earns
on his trading.
[*]You set how much of the profit goes into his gear. Raise it and he arms
himself better while less reaches you; lower it and the caravan pays you well
and its master stays plain. With nothing left to improve, he takes nothing.
[*]Bandits who beat his caravan take him prisoner and strip him, so the better
armed he is, the more a lost caravan costs you.
[/list]

[b]Robbery[/b]
[list]
[*]Talk to a lord you hold prisoner and you can strip him of his arms and armour
and keep them. Unique gear included: take Caladog's gilded armour as a trophy.
[*]Other lords do the same to their prisoners, and the gear stays in the world.
They wear it or sell it, and beating them can win it back.
[*][b]For you, it has a price.[/b] It costs you standing with the man you rob and a
mark against your honour. Robbing a dishonourable prisoner costs half; robbing
a friend costs full.
[*][b]For the AI, it is character.[/b] Whether a captor robs depends on his
personality -- honour above all, then generosity and mercy -- on how he stands
with the prisoner, and on his roguery. Bandits always rob.
[/list]

[h2]How to rob a prisoner[/h2]

[list=1]
[*]Hold a lord prisoner, in your party or in a town or castle your clan owns.
[*]Talk to him: the prisoner list on the party screen, or the dungeon.
[*]Choose [i]"Hand over your arms and armour."[/i]
[/list]

The option only appears if he has something left to take and robbery is on.

[nexus-only]
[h2]Installation[/h2]

[list=1]
[*]Download the archive and drop the [i]HeroesEvolve[/i] folder into
[i]Mount & Blade II Bannerlord/Modules/[/i].
[*]Tick Heroes Evolve in the launcher's module list.
[*]Load order sorts itself. The module declares what it needs, so the launcher
places it after the official modules, and after MCM if you have it.
[/list]

Safe to add to a campaign already under way, and safe to remove from one.

[/nexus-only]
[h2]Requirements[/h2]

[list]
[*]Mount & Blade II: Bannerlord v1.4.8
[*][i]Optional:[/i] Mod Configuration Menu (MCM) v5, for the in-game options
screen. Without it every setting still works, read from [i]settings.xml[/i].
[*]No Harmony, no library mods, nothing else.
[/list]

[h2]Configuration[/h2]

[list]
[*]Every system can be switched off on its own.
[*]In-game with MCM, or in [i]settings.xml[/i] without it. Changes apply
immediately.
[*]Tune how freely lords spend, whose colours they favour at market, how often
they rob, how good their gear gets, what a caravan pays its master, and exclude
items.
[*]Choose whether your own clan is managed by the mod or left entirely to you.
[*]A one-click census writes a survey of every lord in your campaign to a log.
[/list]

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]

[list]
[*]Writes nothing into your save.
[*]Works with mods that add items or cultures.
[*]Built and tested with War Sails. Does not require it.
[/list]

[h2]Shout outs[/h2]

[list]
[*][b]DynamicLordGear[/b] -- the shape of the gear ceiling. The numbers are no
longer its numbers, but grading a lord's kit by his merit is its idea.
[*][b]Lords Gear[/b] -- the starting point for how much of a clan's purse a lord
may put into armour.
[*][b]NoblesBuyStuff[/b] -- for showing that a wallet built from a hero's own
gold leaves most of the map unable to buy anything, which is why there is a clan
purse here at all.
[*][b]Mod Configuration Menu[/b] -- for an options screen worth writing against,
and one you can depend on optionally.
[/list]

[h2]Languages[/h2]

English, Spanish, French, German, Russian, Turkish.
